    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The build passes. The change is complete and the test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented disposable-domain email rejection following the codebase's existing validation conventions (matching how `InvalidTelephoneException` / `InvalidPostcodeException` work):\n\n1. **`rest/advice/DisposableEmailException.java`** (new) \u2014 a `RuntimeException` thrown when an owner's email domain is on the blocklist.\n\n2. **`rest/validation/EmailNormalizer.java`** \u2014 after lower-casing the email to its canonical form, `normalize(...)` now rejects any address whose domain (part after the final `@`) is one of `mailinator.com`, `tempmail.com`, `guerrillamail.com`. Because normalization runs on both create (`normalizeEmail`) and update, the rule is enforced uniformly. A missing/blank email is still left untouched (the field is optional).\n\n3. **`rest/advice/ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`** \u2014 added an `@ExceptionHandler(DisposableEmailException.class)` returning **400 Bad Request** with the `email` field reported in the `errors` array, consistent with the other business-rule 400 handlers.\n\nThe acceptance test `errorRejectsDisposableDomain()` (posts `x@mailinator.com`, expects 400) passes: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
